{
  "id": "praepositionen",
  "slug": "verben-praepositionen",
  "name": "Verbs + prepositions",
  "level": "B1",
  "subtitleKey": "vp.hero.subtitle",
  "subtitle": "Many English verbs go with one fixed preposition: listen to, wait for, depend on, look after, belong to, agree with. Learn the verb and the preposition together, then practise them.",
  "briefMarks": ["listen to", "wait for", "depend on", "look after"],
  "source": "icanspeak Spanish original English text (grammar-b1 run, 2026-10-29)",
  "concept": {
    "flow": [
      {
        "text": "subject"
      },
      {
        "text": "verb",
        "variant": "verb"
      },
      {
        "text": "preposition + object",
        "variant": "prep"
      }
    ],
    "exampleHtml": "Amira <span class=\"vp-verb\">listens</span> <span class=\"vp-prep\">to</span> a podcast on the bus. — Kwame is <span class=\"vp-verb\">waiting</span> <span class=\"vp-prep\">for</span> his friend.",
    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "Some verbs always take the same preposition. The preposition is part of the verb: <em>listen <strong>to</strong>, wait <strong>for</strong>, depend <strong>on</strong>, belong <strong>to</strong>, agree <strong>with</strong>, worry <strong>about</strong></em>. Learn them as one unit. The wrong preposition sounds strange, and a missing one is a mistake.",
        "tag": "Verb + preposition"
      },
      {
        "html": "After the preposition comes a noun, a pronoun (<em>me, you, him, her, it, us, them</em>) or an <em>-ing</em> form: <em>She is waiting for <strong>the bus</strong>. She is waiting for <strong>him</strong>. He is thinking about <strong>moving</strong>.</em> Never use <em>to</em> + verb after these prepositions.",
        "tag": "After the preposition"
      },
      {
        "html": "A different preposition can mean something different: <em>look <strong>at</strong> a photo</em> (use your eyes), <em>look <strong>for</strong> your keys</em> (search), <em>look <strong>after</strong> a child</em> (take care of). Check the preposition when you learn a verb.",
        "tag": "Same verb, new meaning"
      },
      {
        "html": "✗ <em>I'm waiting the bus.</em> → ✓ <em>I'm waiting for the bus.</em><br>✗ <em>She listens music.</em> → ✓ <em>She listens to music.</em><br>✗ <em>It depends of the weather.</em> → ✓ <em>It depends on the weather.</em>",
        "tag": "Typical mistakes"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "A few verbs take <strong>no</strong> preposition in English, even if your language uses one: <em>discuss the plan</em> (not ✗ discuss about), <em>marry someone</em>, <em>enter a room</em>. For questions like <em>What are you waiting for?</em> and answers like <em>I'm waiting for it</em>, see <a href=\"/prepositions-at-the-end\">Prepositions at the end</a>. After a preposition you use the <em>-ing</em> form: <a href=\"/gerunds\">Gerunds</a> and <a href=\"/verb-ing-or-to\">-ing or to</a>. Some verb + preposition pairs can be split from their object, such as <em>give up</em>: those are phrasal verbs, see <a href=\"/phrasal-verbs\">Phrasal verbs</a>.",
    "reference": {
      "showLabel": "Show overview ▼",
      "hideLabel": "Hide overview ▲",
      "tables": [
        {
          "caption": "Common verbs and their prepositions",
          "head": [
            "Preposition",
            "Verbs",
            "Example"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "to",
              "listen to, belong to, talk to, speak to, write to",
              "Priya listens to the radio."
            ],
            [
              "for",
              "wait for, look for, ask for, pay for, search for",
              "We are waiting for a taxi."
            ],
            [
              "on",
              "depend on, rely on, concentrate on",
              "It depends on the weather."
            ],
            [
              "with",
              "agree with, deal with",
              "I agree with you."
            ],
            [
              "about",
              "talk about, think about, worry about, complain about",
              "Don't worry about the exam."
            ],
            [
              "at",
              "look at, laugh at, arrive at (a small place)",
              "Look at this photo!"
            ],
            [
              "in · of",
              "believe in, succeed in · think of, dream of",
              "Mei believes in herself."
            ],
            [
              "after",
              "look after (= take care of)",
              "Zofia looks after her little brother."
            ]
          ]
        }
      ],
      "boxes": [
        {
          "badge": "Look + preposition",
          "variant": "akk",
          "items": [
            "look at = use your eyes: Look at the sky!",
            "look for = search: I'm looking for my keys.",
            "look after = take care of: Tom looks after the dog."
          ]
        },
        {
          "badge": "After the preposition",
          "variant": "dat",
          "items": [
            "noun: She is waiting for the bus.",
            "pronoun: She is waiting for him.",
            "-ing: He is thinking about changing jobs."
          ]
        },
        {
          "badge": "No preposition in English",
          "variant": "akk",
          "items": [
            "discuss the plan (not discuss about)",
            "marry someone (not marry with)",
            "enter a room (not enter in)"
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  },
